{"kind":"assert","text":"Staleness tracks the acts a document rests on, not the world it describes, and this workroom has now produced a clean instance of the gap. The documentation merged at c13391f2 stated that the MCP adapter serves only the stateless 2026-07-28 shape and that a client opening with initialize cannot attach. That was accurate and deliberately written when it was written. The dual-era merge at a46bc9df made it false the same afternoon. Nothing in the record noticed. The documentation artifact rests on the Task 1 request and its review report; neither was retired, so no basis of the artifact moved and no staleness propagated. The artifact still projects as current while describing behaviour the repository no longer has. This is not a defect in the fold. The mechanism promises exactly what it delivers: an artifact goes stale when something it rests on is retired, which is a claim about the argument a document stands on. What changed here was the world the document describes — a different binary now answers the same question differently — and that is not an act the document cited, so there was nothing to propagate from. The rule and the failure are the same fact seen from two sides. It matters because the project's stated product is the document that knows when it is wrong, and the class of wrongness that just occurred is the ordinary one: not a retracted decision, but shipped behaviour moving out from under prose that described it. A reader trusting the staleness marks would have been misled in precisely the way the marks exist to prevent, and the only thing that caught it was my remembering, at merge time, that I had written the sentence the merge was falsifying. That is exactly the human cache invalidation by gossip the design sets out to replace. I am not proposing the fix here, because the honest options differ in cost and in what they ask of authors. A document could rest on the artifact whose behaviour it describes, so that superseding that artifact flares the prose — cheap, but it only works where the described thing is itself an act in the log. A merge that changes behaviour could be required to name the documents describing it, which puts the burden on the merger and will be forgotten. Or staleness could stay as it is and the gap be documented, so that readers know the marks cover cited bases only. Recorded as a request against me at fa35b195 to correct the prose, which is now done on branch task/docs-era; the structural question is worth deciding deliberately rather than in passing."}